---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Rules" description="What every implementation on DS with X may and may not use.">
  <h1>Rules</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every implementation may use its language and that language's standard library, and nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>No third-party packages</h2>
  <p>
    Python may use <code>csv</code>, <code>math</code>, and <code>statistics</code>, but not NumPy, pandas, or
    scikit-learn. Ruby may use its standard library, but no gems. The containers that run the tests contain
    only the language's own toolchain and have no network access, so nothing else can sneak in.
  </p>

  <h2>Built-in features are allowed, and labeled</h2>
  <p>
    Some tools ship data science features of their own, such as PostgreSQL's <code>regr_slope</code>, R's{' '}
    <code>lm()</code>, or BigQuery ML. A page may show the built-in version beside a from-scratch one, and
    each is labeled <span class="badge">From scratch</span> or <span class="badge">Built-in</span>.
  </p>

  <h2>One shared dataset per concept</h2>
  <p>
    Every implementation of a concept reads the same <code>data.csv</code> and must print the same results,
    to within a relative tolerance of 10⁻⁴ (or an absolute tolerance of 10⁻⁶, whichever is looser).
  </p>

  <h2>Every implementation is verified</h2>
  <p>
    Each one is either run by the test harness on every change, marked{' '}
    <span class="badge pass">Tested automatically</span>, or, for tools that cannot run in a container, such
    as cloud services, checked by hand and marked <span class="badge warn">Manually verified</span> with the
    date. The code you see is the exact file that was run.
  </p>

  <h2>Clarity over cleverness</h2>
  <p>
    Code is written to teach: idiomatic for its language, commented where the language is unfamiliar, and
    short enough to read on one page where possible.
  </p>
</Base>
